# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored and must stay that way: this repo is a fork
# of a public upstream, so anything committed here is world-readable.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Resolution order for every key below is: real environment -> this file. That is the whole
# list. influx_source.py used to fall back to ../../alphaess-collector/.env as well, so a Mac
# checkout beside that repo needed no token of its own; that step is gone. This repo should
# not be reading another one's private file, the path was a guess about directory layout, and
# once the collector split its admin token into four scoped ones the fallback would have
# quietly returned the admin one.
#
# So every machine needs its own copy of this file, including the dev Mac.


# --- InfluxDB, fed by alphaess-collector -------------------------------------------------
# The planner refuses to run without SOC, so a missing token is a hard stop, not a warning.

# Where InfluxDB is. Use ONE of these two forms.
#   from a container on the NAS, over the shared docker network:
INFLUX_URL=http://influxdb:8086
#   from a laptop on the LAN, talking to the NAS directly (INFLUX_PORT defaults to 8086):
#INFLUX_HOST=192.168.68.105
#INFLUX_PORT=8086

# alphaess-collector issues four narrowly-scoped tokens rather than one admin token. Ours is
# INFLUX_TOKEN_PLANNING: read on the actuals bucket, write on the plan bucket
#   read:alphaess + write:planning
#
# Both names below are read and the specific one wins, so the value can be copied out of the
# collector's .env under its own name and left alone. Set one of them - the container refuses
# to start with neither.
INFLUX_TOKEN_PLANNING=
#INFLUX_TOKEN=

INFLUX_ORG=home

# Actuals written by the collector: SOC, load, PV. Read only.
INFLUX_BUCKET=alphaess

# Where every live plan is stored, one series per run. Kept apart from the actuals so a bug
# here cannot touch measured history, and the scoped token enforces that: read on alphaess,
# write on planning. The bucket carries a 400-day retention, set on the collector side.
# BT_WRITE_PLAN=N in the environment turns the writing off without changing anything else.
#INFLUX_PLAN_BUCKET=planning

# Optional. Only needed if more than one AlphaESS system reports into the same bucket.
#ALPHAESS_SYS_SN=

# Sampling interval the collector runs at. Used to judge whether an hour is fully covered,
# so a collector outage is rejected instead of reading as a very quiet house.
#POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30


# --- Container (docker-compose.yml only) --------------------------------------------------
# Nothing to set. The container starts as root, reads the owner off the ./data bind mount,
# becomes that user and refuses to start if it still cannot write there. Create the directory
# as yourself before the first run - `mkdir -p data` - and the files it produces will belong
# to you.
#
# These two are the fallback for the one case with no answer to read: docker had to create
# ./data itself, so it is owned by root and no host user has a claim on it.
#PLANNER_UID=1000
#PLANNER_GID=1000

# Port the report-viewer container publishes on the NAS host. data/reports/*.txt
# (report_day.py's daily narrative) is browsable at http://<nas-ip>:<this>/.
#REPORT_VIEWER_PORT=8091

# Where the irreplaceable archive lives on the host - the APsystems EMA year and the Sparky
# P1/P4 export, mounted read-only at /archive. Only the backtest scripts read it; the planning
# path never touches it.
#
# The default is the verified copy, and the dated directory is deliberately part of the path:
# a new archive should appear beside the old one and be selected here, not overwrite it.
# It must NOT live under /volume1/docker/ - a stack rebuild can reach that.
#ARCHIVE_DIR=/volume1/documents/battery-archive/battery-archive-20260730


# --- Weather (weather_source.py) ------------------------------------------------------------
# Where to fetch weather for. Defaults to Almere centre, 52.3708/5.2158. This is NOT the same
# as the planner's siteLatitude/siteLongitude (52.5/5.5 in Marstek-planning.py): that pair only
# feeds sun elevation, where 15 km is a fraction of a degree, while cloud cover 15 km away over
# open water is a different sky.
#
# Open-Meteo needs no key and no account. Nothing in the planning path reads this data - it is
# stored so that a temperature term in the load forecast can one day be judged on evidence.
#WEATHER_LAT=52.3708
#WEATHER_LON=5.2158


# --- KNMI ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not used by any code yet. Parked here for the PV-forecast work in NAS-DEPLOYMENT-PLAN.md
# ("A better PV forecast source"). Get one from dataplatform.knmi.nl:
#   API Catalog -> Open Data API -> "Request an API key". Shown once, so save it here.
#KNMI_API_KEY=
